\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Borel sets of real fields}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{A \newvocab{sigma-algebra} on a set #{X} is a family of subsets
containing #{X} and closed under complements and countable unions.
It is also closed under countable intersections, by taking complements.
The \newvocab{Borel sigma-algebra} #{\mathcal B(X)} of a topological
space is the smallest sigma-algebra containing all its open sets;
see \citet{Definition 1.4.16, p. 87, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.}
\p{On the [real field space](fdip-002C), define the coordinate-product
sigma-algebra #{\Sigma_V} to be generated by all cylinders
#{\{x:x(v)\in B\}}, where #{v\in V} and #{B\in\mathcal B(\mathbb R)}.
This is the product construction of
\citet{Section 1.7.4, pp. 194-195, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.
The [Borel-product proposition](fdip-002E) identifies it with
#{\mathcal B(X_V)}. We use this Borel structure without completion:
no additional subsets of null sets are adjoined.}
\p{For the singleton #{X_\varnothing}, the Borel structure is
#{\{\varnothing,X_\varnothing\}}. A product of completed factor
sigma-algebras need not be the completed product sigma-algebra; these
constructions are distinguished in
\citet{p. 94 and Exercise 1.7.19, p. 195, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.}
